munication), egocentric, not willing to further education (adaptability). Also calm in crisis? Not in our notes, but Agent 2 earlier said C keeps calm in crisis? Actually that was from Agent 1's earlier note: \"C keeps calm in a crisis, puts concern for others above everything.\" Wait that was in Agent 1's internal notes, not in our notes. Our notes do not include that. We must not use facts not in our notes. So we cannot claim C keeps calm in crisis. We have only the facts listed.\n\nCandidate A: anticipate dangerous situations (decision), see complex connections (technical), excellent spatial vision (technical), very good leadership qualities (leadership), but can be unorganized (reliability), not open to new ideas (adaptability). Also no cooperation info.\n\nThus we need to decide which candidate seems best overall. D has strong reliability and responsibility but cooperation concerns. B has strong stress resilience and reliability but cooperation concerns too. C has cooperation positive atmosphere, decision quality, technical, but communication issues and egocentric, not willing to further education. A has technical and leadership but organization issues.\n\nWe might argue that Candidate C's positive crew atmosphere and decision quality outweigh communication issues, and that his egocentric and education reluctance are concerns but perhaps less critical than D's hot temper and weak leadership. Or we could argue B's stress handling and conscientiousness make him strong, but his uncooperative behavior and nasty remarks are severe for crew.\n\nWe need to reference existing discussion: Agent 1 and 2 focusing on D. We can either support D or propose an alternative. Perhaps we can argue that Candidate B's stress resilience and conscientiousness make him a stronger fit, despite cooperation concerns, but we need to compare.\n\nWe have explicit facts: B handles stress very well, very conscientious. Cooperation concerns: can be uncooperative, makes nasty remarks. D's cooperation concerns: know-it-all, moody, hot temper, weak leadership. Which is worse? Both are concerning. But D also has weak leadership and hot temper, which could be more severe for long-distance flights. B's uncooperative and nasty remarks also severe.\n\nWe could argue that B's stress resilience is a critical criterion, and D lacks clear evidence of calmness under high pressure (only \"adequate response to unexpected events\"). So B may be better on stress resilience. Also B's conscientiousness indicates reliability. D's reliability is good but cooperation concerns bigger.\n\nThus we could shift recommendation to B.\n\nWe need to reference the discussion: Agent 2 pointed out D's stress resilience gap. We can use that to argue B is stronger.\n\nWe must include explicit facts: B handles stress very well; B very conscientious. Also note B's cooperation concerns but compare to D's hot temper and weak leadership.\n\nThus our public message: I am less convinced by D given lack of strong stress resilience evidence and cooperation concerns; I would recommend B as the best overall fit.\n\nWe need to include vote in metadata JSON: \"Candidate B\".\n\nProceed."}
